WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.890
Welcome to this Java tutorial on decisions and

00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:07.167
Boolean logic, or true-or-false reasoning, where

00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:10.034
you'll compare values, combine conditions,

00:00:10.034 --> 00:00:13.106
choose one possible path called a branch, and

00:00:13.106 --> 00:00:15.837
test where a program's decision changes.

00:00:16.628 --> 00:00:20.058
Comparisons and logical conditions help software

00:00:20.058 --> 00:00:23.416
apply booking and borrowing rules consistently,

00:00:23.416 --> 00:00:26.774
while boundary tests catch mistakes where those

00:00:26.774 --> 00:00:27.703
rules change.

00:00:28.437 --> 00:00:31.262
Today we'll build a campus event decision using

00:00:31.262 --> 00:00:33.906
a visitor's age in whole years and a Boolean

00:00:33.906 --> 00:00:36.790
fact recording whether the visitor has a student

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:36.971
ID.

00:00:36.971 --> 00:00:39.435
Under this example's rules, visitors aged

00:00:39.435 --> 00:00:41.838
eighteen or older with an ID receive the

00:00:41.838 --> 00:00:43.401
five-dollar student price.

00:00:43.401 --> 00:00:45.925
Others who meet that age limit receive the

00:00:45.925 --> 00:00:48.810
eight-dollar general price, and younger visitors

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.312
are directed to the desk.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.810
The program should display exactly one admission

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.999
instruction for the supplied visitor.

00:00:55.999 --> 00:00:58.602
We'll first work through equipment borrowing

00:00:58.602 --> 00:01:01.146
rules, where we can inspect comparisons and

00:01:01.146 --> 00:01:02.092
follow a choice.

00:01:02.092 --> 00:01:04.755
Then you'll use that reasoning to predict the

00:01:04.755 --> 00:01:06.115
event program's result.

00:01:06.115 --> 00:01:08.896
The supplied facts are repeatable examples; the

00:01:08.896 --> 00:01:11.322
program doesn't verify an ID or collect a

00:01:11.322 --> 00:01:11.795
payment.

00:01:11.879 --> 00:01:14.521
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace.

00:01:14.521 --> 00:01:17.718
We'll start by making the yes-or-no results of

00:01:17.718 --> 00:01:20.916
our borrowing rules visible before we use them

00:01:20.916 --> 00:01:22.654
to choose an instruction.

00:01:39.822 --> 00:01:42.765
At our equipment desk, the training requirement

00:01:42.765 --> 00:01:44.831
is at least four completed hours.

00:01:44.831 --> 00:01:47.461
This borrower has four hours and no badge.

00:01:47.461 --> 00:01:50.216
A condition is an expression that produces a

00:01:50.216 --> 00:01:52.094
Boolean result: true or false.

00:01:52.094 --> 00:01:54.599
We'll compare the stored count with that

00:01:54.599 --> 00:01:55.977
inclusive lower limit.

00:01:55.977 --> 00:01:58.732
A comparison evaluates the current value; it

00:01:58.732 --> 00:02:00.297
doesn't change the hours.

00:02:04.714 --> 00:02:07.612
The greater-than-or-equal operator includes the

00:02:07.612 --> 00:02:08.414
limit itself.

00:02:08.414 --> 00:02:10.757
Greater than alone would exclude four.

00:02:10.757 --> 00:02:13.532
Less-than operators test the other direction.

00:02:13.532 --> 00:02:16.491
These size comparisons use relational operators.

00:02:16.491 --> 00:02:19.081
For numeric equality, Java uses two equals

00:02:19.081 --> 00:02:21.486
signs; one equals sign assigns a value.

00:02:21.486 --> 00:02:24.384
Let's now combine the training requirement with

00:02:24.384 --> 00:02:25.864
the separate badge fact.

00:02:41.902 --> 00:02:43.843
AND requires both facts to be true.

00:02:43.843 --> 00:02:46.338
OR requires at least one, including when both

00:02:46.338 --> 00:02:46.837
are true.

00:02:46.837 --> 00:02:48.445
NOT reverses a Boolean value.

00:02:48.445 --> 00:02:50.996
The badge variable already supplies a Boolean,

00:02:50.996 --> 00:02:52.437
so we can use it directly.

00:02:52.437 --> 00:02:54.766
Now consider ten kits shared equally among

00:02:54.766 --> 00:02:55.154
groups.

00:02:55.154 --> 00:02:57.816
We'll use divisor for the group count, currently

00:02:57.816 --> 00:03:00.256
zero, and check whether each group gets more

00:03:00.256 --> 00:03:01.032
than two kits.

00:03:01.032 --> 00:03:03.583
Before attempting that division, we must check

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:05.912
the group count to avoid dividing by zero.

00:03:05.912 --> 00:03:08.351
The AND condition asks whether a valid group

00:03:08.351 --> 00:03:11.013
count gives more than two kits per group; the OR

00:03:11.013 --> 00:03:13.619
condition flags either a missing group count or

00:03:13.619 --> 00:03:15.227
more than two kits per group.

00:03:30.262 --> 00:03:33.020
The last two conditions show another feature of

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:33.665
AND and OR.

00:03:33.665 --> 00:03:36.247
Integer division by zero cannot produce this

00:03:36.247 --> 00:03:36.775
quotient.

00:03:36.775 --> 00:03:39.533
In the AND expression, the left check is false,

00:03:39.533 --> 00:03:41.469
so both requirements cannot hold.

00:03:41.469 --> 00:03:44.286
In the OR expression, the left check is true, so

00:03:44.286 --> 00:03:45.870
at least one already holds.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:48.628
Java can skip the division in both expressions.

00:03:48.628 --> 00:03:51.093
Let's click Run to inspect all six Boolean

00:03:51.093 --> 00:03:51.562
results.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:59.095
The training comparison is true at four hours.

00:03:59.095 --> 00:04:01.528
AND is false because the badge requirement

00:04:01.528 --> 00:04:04.309
fails; OR is true because training is sufficient

00:04:04.309 --> 00:04:05.699
for that different rule.

00:04:05.699 --> 00:04:08.133
NOT turns the false badge value into true.

00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:10.855
The final false and true results appear without

00:04:10.855 --> 00:04:12.130
an attempted division.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:14.911
This is short-circuit evaluation: Java skips the

00:04:14.911 --> 00:04:17.518
right side when the left side already settles

00:04:17.518 --> 00:04:18.155
the result.

00:04:18.217 --> 00:04:20.657
That shortcut depends on the operator and the

00:04:20.657 --> 00:04:20.982
order.

00:04:20.982 --> 00:04:23.422
A true left side of AND still needs its right

00:04:23.422 --> 00:04:25.862
side; a false left side of OR still needs its

00:04:25.862 --> 00:04:26.458
right side.

00:04:26.458 --> 00:04:28.790
With a group count of five, the first guard

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:31.284
would permit the division and then compare its

00:04:31.284 --> 00:04:32.260
quotient with two.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:34.808
Placing the unsafe division first would attempt

00:04:34.808 --> 00:04:36.489
it before the protective check.

00:04:36.489 --> 00:04:38.766
Now let's use a complete rule to choose an

00:04:38.766 --> 00:04:39.417
instruction.

00:04:59.759 --> 00:05:01.991
Our equipment desk has three choices.

00:05:01.991 --> 00:05:04.886
Four or more training hours with a badge permits

00:05:04.886 --> 00:05:05.731
self checkout.

00:05:05.731 --> 00:05:08.506
Enough training without a badge requires staff

00:05:08.506 --> 00:05:09.048
checkout.

00:05:09.048 --> 00:05:11.944
Less training requires the training instruction.

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:14.719
We'll store one instruction and print it after

00:05:14.719 --> 00:05:15.382
the choice.

00:05:15.382 --> 00:05:18.278
Declaring that name before the branches makes it

00:05:18.278 --> 00:05:19.484
available afterward.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:32.537
Each possible path is a conditional branch.

00:05:32.537 --> 00:05:35.018
The keyword if introduces a condition in

00:05:35.018 --> 00:05:35.762
parentheses.

00:05:35.762 --> 00:05:38.429
If the condition is true, Java executes the

00:05:38.429 --> 00:05:40.972
statements in its block, the group inside

00:05:40.972 --> 00:05:41.406
braces.

00:05:41.406 --> 00:05:44.383
Here the first choice requires both training and

00:05:44.383 --> 00:05:44.879
a badge.

00:05:44.879 --> 00:05:47.856
We'll connect the other choices with else if and

00:05:47.856 --> 00:05:50.337
a final else, so they belong to the same

00:05:50.337 --> 00:05:50.895
decision.

00:06:09.051 --> 00:06:11.893
Else if supplies another condition after the

00:06:11.893 --> 00:06:13.314
earlier one was false.

00:06:13.314 --> 00:06:15.963
The final else covers the remaining case.

00:06:15.963 --> 00:06:18.999
One connected chain executes the first matching

00:06:18.999 --> 00:06:20.808
branch and skips the others.

00:06:20.808 --> 00:06:23.714
Every path assigns instruction, and the print

00:06:23.714 --> 00:06:26.169
after the chain uses that chosen text.

00:06:26.169 --> 00:06:29.076
Let's click Run with four hours and no badge.

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:35.988
The program prints Staff checkout.

00:06:35.988 --> 00:06:38.650
Four hours meets the training limit, but the

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.555
missing badge makes the first combined condition

00:06:41.555 --> 00:06:41.918
false.

00:06:41.918 --> 00:06:44.642
The next training comparison succeeds, so its

00:06:44.642 --> 00:06:45.610
assignment runs.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:47.305
The final branch is skipped.

00:06:47.305 --> 00:06:50.209
Execution then continues after the choice to the

00:06:50.209 --> 00:06:53.054
print statement; choosing a branch does not end

00:06:53.054 --> 00:06:53.780
the program.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:56.648
Scope is the region of source code where a

00:06:56.648 --> 00:06:58.312
declared name can be used.

00:06:58.312 --> 00:07:01.320
Instruction was declared before this choice, so

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.664
the print can use it.

00:07:02.664 --> 00:07:05.736
A new variable declared inside a branch's braces

00:07:05.736 --> 00:07:07.592
would be local to that block.

00:07:07.592 --> 00:07:10.408
Entering the branch would not make that name

00:07:10.408 --> 00:07:13.160
available outside its source-code boundary.

00:07:13.339 --> 00:07:16.224
Now let's test the other side of the training

00:07:16.224 --> 00:07:16.609
limit.

00:07:16.609 --> 00:07:19.558
We'll change only the hours from four to three

00:07:19.558 --> 00:07:21.161
and keep the badge false.

00:07:21.161 --> 00:07:24.046
A boundary test checks a value at or near the

00:07:24.046 --> 00:07:26.161
point where the decision changes.

00:07:26.161 --> 00:07:29.239
Testing four and three can expose a mistake that

00:07:29.239 --> 00:07:31.739
testing a larger hour count would hide.

00:07:37.895 --> 00:07:41.187
The same chain is ready with the changed input.

00:07:41.187 --> 00:07:44.479
Let's click Run and check which instruction the

00:07:44.479 --> 00:07:46.020
smaller count selects.

00:07:51.379 --> 00:07:53.885
The result changes to Complete training.

00:07:53.885 --> 00:07:56.579
Three fails the training comparison, so the

00:07:56.579 --> 00:07:59.211
first branch is unavailable and the second

00:07:59.211 --> 00:08:00.777
comparison is also false.

00:08:00.777 --> 00:08:03.283
The final else supplies the instruction.

00:08:03.283 --> 00:08:06.165
We've checked both sides of this limit without

00:08:06.165 --> 00:08:07.293
changing the rule.

00:08:07.293 --> 00:08:10.237
The notebook will ask you to test both relevant

00:08:10.237 --> 00:08:11.929
facts in its event example.

00:08:12.725 --> 00:08:15.734
Let's replace the borrowing program with the

00:08:15.734 --> 00:08:16.692
event program.

00:08:16.692 --> 00:08:19.496
For this visitor, age is nineteen and the

00:08:19.496 --> 00:08:21.138
student-ID fact is true.

00:08:21.138 --> 00:08:24.421
Begin with those two facts and the most specific

00:08:24.421 --> 00:08:27.635
condition, which requires both adulthood and an

00:08:27.635 --> 00:08:27.841
ID.

00:08:27.841 --> 00:08:30.850
Then we'll attach the event's other choices.

00:09:05.896 --> 00:09:08.755
The first branch prints the student price in

00:09:08.755 --> 00:09:10.965
dollars under this example's rule.

00:09:10.965 --> 00:09:13.889
If that combined condition is false, the next

00:09:13.889 --> 00:09:17.008
condition checks the age requirement for general

00:09:17.008 --> 00:09:17.658
admission.

00:09:17.658 --> 00:09:20.257
The final else handles younger visitors.

00:09:20.257 --> 00:09:23.376
Let's attach those alternatives and complete the

00:09:23.376 --> 00:09:23.896
program.

00:09:46.873 --> 00:09:49.740
Before we run, write the exact message you

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:51.379
expect for this visitor.

00:09:51.379 --> 00:09:54.655
Start at the first condition, use the stored age

00:09:54.655 --> 00:09:57.386
and ID value, and decide whether a later

00:09:57.386 --> 00:09:58.819
condition is reached.

00:09:58.819 --> 00:10:01.209
Also predict how many lines appear.

00:10:01.209 --> 00:10:03.598
Pause here if you'd like more time.

00:10:11.906 --> 00:10:15.150
Let's click Run and compare the actual admission

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:17.381
instruction with your prediction.

00:10:22.707 --> 00:10:25.254
One line appears: Student admission, five.

00:10:25.254 --> 00:10:28.043
Nineteen meets the inclusive age limit and the

00:10:28.043 --> 00:10:30.651
ID value is true, so the first branch runs.

00:10:30.651 --> 00:10:33.501
The later branches are skipped, even though the

00:10:33.501 --> 00:10:35.866
age-only comparison would also be true.

00:10:35.866 --> 00:10:38.413
Separate if statements would make separate

00:10:38.413 --> 00:10:40.657
decisions and could print conflicting

00:10:40.657 --> 00:10:43.507
instructions; this connected chain selects one.

00:10:44.549 --> 00:10:46.954
We've turned supplied facts into Boolean

00:10:46.954 --> 00:10:49.178
results, matched logical operators to

00:10:49.178 --> 00:10:51.703
requirements, and used an ordered chain to

00:10:51.703 --> 00:10:52.785
choose one action.

00:10:52.785 --> 00:10:55.610
Braces grouped the branch statements, and local

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:58.255
scope controlled where names were available.

00:10:58.255 --> 00:11:00.660
Short-circuit evaluation explained which

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:02.283
expressions were attempted.

00:11:02.283 --> 00:11:05.168
Boundary tests checked where the selected result

00:11:05.168 --> 00:11:05.649
changed.

00:11:06.586 --> 00:11:09.179
Now construct the notebook's admission program

00:11:09.179 --> 00:11:11.771
from the rules, starting with age eighteen and

00:11:11.771 --> 00:11:12.560
no student ID.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.209
Predict all four combinations of ages seventeen

00:11:15.209 --> 00:11:17.238
and eighteen with the two ID values.

00:11:17.238 --> 00:11:19.718
Change only the inputs for each run, compare

00:11:19.718 --> 00:11:22.423
every complete message with your prediction, and

00:11:22.423 --> 00:11:24.452
check that exactly one line appears.

00:11:24.452 --> 00:11:26.932
Restore the initial eighteen and no-ID case,

00:11:26.932 --> 00:11:28.961
then explain what each test checked.
